{
  "opportunity_id": "proc_fce6d9475e",
  "source": "content_cluster",
  "title": "Meter-level scheduled-vs-actual variance clearing across Unify, Sitara and MOPS",
  "task_class": "volume_imbalance_reconciliation",
  "label_mechanical": "Volume and imbalance reconciliation: reconcile volumes / scheduled / actual",
  "narrative": {
    "what_happens": "An exception surfaces on a meter \u2014 a variance report, a \"meters with flow and no nom\" list, or a counterparty saying a scheduled volume actualized at zero \u2014 and someone emails the meter number, the affected days and the two numbers to whoever they believe owns that point. The recipient checks flow against the nomination, and where they agree a change is made by hand in the system of record: volumes corrected in MOPS, estimated volumes adjusted in Unify to match what HPL shows, or a meter reallocated so total flow lands on one counterparty. Answers come back day by day and contract by contract rather than as a file, so the requester re-keys them and mails a confirmation. A thread closes when both sides agree the numbers and the exception drops off the requester's list; several close only with a stated temporary fix, leaving the underlying allocation unresolved.",
    "where_it_stalls": "Half of these messages carry rework and roughly a third carry waiting, with escalation the second most common role: threads stall because the person holding the exception cannot resolve it alone and must wait on a volume-management or measurement counterpart who replies partially or not at all. Several messages show the chase and the misroute explicitly (\"Sherlyn needs answers....... I dropped the ball and sent this to Carlos\", \"Why is nothing being allocated to Alpine? ... I really need the problem resolved\"), and one shows ownership itself is unclear \u2014 the daily imbalance check is being covered only when its usual owner is out. Because agreement is reached in prose and then typed into Unify or MOPS by hand, the same variance is worked more than once.",
    "why_it_recurs": "Measurement and actualization always land after scheduling, so every month produces a fresh exception list per meter, and the systems of record \u2014 Unify, Sitara, MOPS, HPL's figures \u2014 do not agree with each other automatically.",
    "automation_opportunity": "A scheduled comparison that joins scheduled, confirmed and actualized volumes per meter and day, publishes the exception list with the named owner of each meter attached, and accepts the agreed correction as a posted adjustment rather than an email that someone re-keys into Unify or MOPS.",
    "coherence": "tight"
  },
